        "titulo": "Gender traps 2: masculines in -а/-я, «дитя», two genders and loanwords",
        "explicacao": "STEP 1: TRAP No. 4 — Nouns in -а/-я that are masculine. Not every word ending in -а or -я is feminine. There are exceptions, and they matter because they name males. па́па (PAH-pah) — dad де́душка (DYEH-doosh-kah) — grandpa дя́дя (DYAH-dya) — uncle мужчи́на (moozh-CHEE-nah) — man ю́ноша (YOO-noh-shah) — young man судья́ (soo-DYAH) — judge ста́роста (STAH-rah-stah) — group leader, class monitor во́евода (VOH-ee-vah-dah) — military commander (historical) влади́ка (vlah-DEE-kah) — lord, sovereign Ники́та (nee-KEE-tah) — Nikita (male name) Илья́ (ee-LYAH) — Ilya (male name) Лёва (LYOH-vah) — Liova (short for Lev) Са́ша (SAH-shah) — Sasha (short for Aleksandr or Aleksandra) Же́ня (ZHEH-nyah) — Zhenia (short for Evgenii or Evgeniia) All of them end in -а or -я, but they are masculine, because they name men. The ending rule holds for most words, but meaning wins for living beings. It is an exception you use every day, so remember it. STEP 2: TRAP No. 5 — дитя, the child. Дитя́ (dee-TYAH) — child. It is the only neuter word that names a person. All the other words that name people are masculine or feminine. Дитя is the single exception. It is worth knowing because it is a common word. STEP 3: TRAP No. 6 — Nouns of two genders. Some words can be masculine or feminine, depending on the sex of the person they name: сирота́ (see-rah-TAH) — orphan (boy or girl) у́мница (OOM-nee-tsah) — clever boy or girl пья́ница (PYAH-nee-tsah) — drunkard (man or woman) о́бжора (OHB-zhah-rah) — glutton (man or woman) бродя́га (brah-DYAH-gah) — vagabond (man or woman) самоуби́йца (sah-mah-oo-BEE-tsah) — a person who takes their own life (man or woman) The word does not change; the adjective that goes with it reveals the gender. But that is a topic for the next lesson. STEP 4: TRAP No. 7 — Loanwords. Foreign words that enter Russian sometimes do not follow the rules. The main ones: Ending in -и, -у, -ю → usually neuter: такси́ (tahk-SEE; taxi), меню́ (myeh-NYOO; menu), киви́ (KEE-vee; kiwi), желе́ (zheh-LEH; jelly), кафе́ (kah-FEH; café, but careful: кофе is masculine and кафе is neuter!), метро́ (meh-TROH; metro), кино́ (kee-NOH; cinema), пальто́ (pahl-TOH; coat), бюро́ (byoo-ROH; office) Naming women → feminine, whatever the ending: ле́ди (lady), Мэ́ри (Mary), Ма́ргарет (Margaret), Э́лен (Ellen). Animals are masculine: кенгуру́ (kyen-goo-ROO; kangaroo), по́ни (pony) Ending in -о and naming males → masculine: маэ́стро (maestro), импреса́рио (impresario). Place names in -о, such as Токи́о (Tokyo), are neuter. Each case is its own case. But the most common is: a loanword ending in a vowel other than -а/-я → neuter.",
