        "titulo": "THE BIG DIVERGENCE: English vs. Russian",
        "explicacao": "THE BIG DIVERGENCE: English vs. Russian. Here lives the number one confusion for English speakers. It is rarely explained clearly, so let us go straight to the point: in English, things have no gender. A table, a house, a book are all «it». In Russian every noun is masculine, feminine or neuter, and it does not matter what the thing is. See the table. On the left, the English word and what English calls it. On the right, the Russian word and its gender. Notice that there is nothing to «match», because English has no grammatical gender to match. The doctrine of this lesson in one sentence: in Russian, gender belongs to the word, not to the thing. Дом is masculine because in Russian it is masculine. Full stop. This is not a detail. It is the difference between speaking Russian and speaking English with Russian words. You are studying another language. Its rules are its own, not yours.",
