        "titulo": "Gender from the ending",
        "explicacao": "Look at the last letter of the noun in the singular. Masculine: ends in a consonant (стол, дом), in -й (музей) or in -ь (день, словарь). Feminine: ends in -а or -я (книга, неделя), or in -ь (ночь, тетрадь). Neuter: ends in -о, -е or -мя (окно, море, имя, время). Step by step: (1) look at the last letter; (2) if it is -ь, check the list or the dictionary; (3) check whether there is a suffix that gives the gender away (-тель, -ость, -ие...). Two cautions: (1) words in -ь can be masculine or feminine and there is no rule, so memorise the gender with the word; (2) men whose word ends in -а or -я are masculine: папа, дедушка, дядя, мужчина.",
        "comparacao": "English never makes you learn gender, but Russian almost always shows it in the ending, so you can read it off the word. Careful: Russian gender has nothing to do with the thing itself (книга, a book, is feminine; письмо, a letter, is neuter).",
